## X2 — reloption registration

`src/am/ec_distann/options.rs:1364-1374` registers
`covering_payload_attnums` via `pg_sys::add_local_string_reloption` with
`default_val = ptr::null()`, `validator = None`, `filler = None`. This is
byte-for-byte the same registration shape as the three pre-existing string
reloptions in the same block:

- `neighbor_code_format` — `options.rs:1331-1340`
- `source_identity` — `options.rs:1341-1350`
- `head_construction` — `options.rs:1352-1362`

Struct layout is covered:

    src/am/ec_distann/options.rs:188-208
      #[repr(C)]
      struct EcDistannReloptions { ... covering_payload_attnums_offset: i32 }

    src/am/ec_distann/options.rs:1224
      pg_sys::init_local_reloptions(&mut relopts, size_of::<EcDistannReloptions>());

The blob size is derived from `size_of::<EcDistannReloptions>()`, so adding the
offset field cannot write past the allocation. `#[repr(C)]` is present, and the
offsets passed to PostgreSQL come from `offset_of!` on the same struct, so field
ordering is not load-bearing.

Absence semantics:

    src/am/common/reloptions.rs:22-51
      fn read_string_reloption(&self, offset, am_name, name) -> Option<String> {
          if offset == 0 { return None; }
          ...
          if value.is_empty() { pgrx::error!("... value must not be empty"); }
          Some(value.to_owned())
      }

A NULL default means PostgreSQL leaves offset 0 when the option is unspecified,
so absence yields `None` and `EcDistannOptions.covering_payload_attnums` stays
`None`. `relation_options` (`options.rs:1465-1471`) also returns
`EcDistannOptions::DEFAULT` when the relation carries no reloptions blob at all,
and `DEFAULT.covering_payload_attnums` is `None` (`options.rs:363`).

Empty-string note: `read_string_reloption` errors on `''` before
`parse_covering_payload_attnums` ever sees it, so the `raw.is_empty()` arm at
`options.rs:439-441` is unreachable from SQL. It is still directly unit-tested.


## X3 — reloption grammar against the accepted contract

Accepted contract, packet 001 `request.md:24-28`:

    "The value is a canonical comma-separated list of positive physical
     attnums, strictly increasing, unique, and bounded to 16."

Implementation `options.rs:432-467` enforces, in order:

1. non-empty (`:439-441`)
2. token count <= `DISTANN_PAYLOAD_COVER_MAX_ATTRIBUTES` (`:442-445`)
3. each token non-empty and all-ASCII-digit (`:449-451`) — rejects
   whitespace, `+`, `-`, and any sign or separator variant
4. `parse::<u16>()` (`:452-454`) — rejects `65536` and above
5. `attnum != 0` (`:455-457`)
6. `token == attnum.to_string()` (`:458-460`) — rejects leading zeros,
   which is the canonical-spelling requirement
7. `attnum > previous` (`:461-463`) — strictly increasing implies unique

Rejection order 3-before-4 matters and is correct: `parse::<u16>` would
otherwise accept nothing extra, but the digit gate makes the `+2` rejection
explicit rather than incidental.

Unit coverage `options.rs:1500-1519` asserts `"1,3,16"` accepts and that
`""`, `"0"`, `"01"`, `"1,"`, `",1"`, `"1, 2"`, `"2,1"`, `"1,1"`, `"65536"`,
`"1,+2"` and a 17-element list all reject. That is one case per rejection arm
above plus the bound.

Gate on `distributed_control`, `options.rs:1439-1443`:

    if covering_payload_attnums.is_some() && !reloptions.distributed_control {
        pgrx::error!("... a payload cover requires distributed_control=true");
    }

Validator timing: no `validate_string_relopt` callback is registered, so the
grammar is enforced when reloptions are *read*, not when the catalog row is
written. For `CREATE INDEX` this is transactionally equivalent — `ambuild`
reads options and the failing statement rolls back. For
`ALTER INDEX ... SET (...)` the bad value commits and every later
`relation_options()` call on that index errors. That is the pre-existing
posture of `neighbor_code_format`, `source_identity`, and `head_construction`
(all `validator = None`, all `pgrx::error!` at parse time in `to_options`), so
this is consistent with the AM, not a new hazard introduced here.


## X4 — fixed PG18 scalar allowlist and widths

`src/am/ec_distann/payload_sidecar.rs:27-39`:

    fn fixed_binary_width(attribute) -> Option<u16> {
        if attribute.type_namespace != "pg_catalog" { return None; }
        match attribute.type_name.as_str() {
            "bool" => Some(1),
            "int2" => Some(2),
            "int4" | "float4" | "date" => Some(4),
            "int8" | "float8" | "time" | "timestamp" | "timestamptz" => Some(8),
            "uuid" => Some(16),
            _ => None,
        }
    }

Set comparison against packet 001 `request.md:36-38`
(`bool`, `int2`, `int4`, `int8`, `float4`, `float8`, `uuid`, `date`, `time`,
`timestamp`, `timestamptz`): set-identical, 11 for 11, nothing added, nothing
dropped.

Widths checked against the PG18 binary send output length for each type:
`bool_send` 1, `int2send` 2, `int4send`/`float4send`/`date_send` 4,
`int8send`/`float8send`/`time_send`/`timestamp_send`/`timestamptz_send` 8,
`uuid_send` 16. Every entry matches. `timetz` (12 bytes) and `interval`
(16 bytes, 3 fields) are correctly absent.

Exclusion of domain / user-defined / array types is structural, not a
denylist. `resolve_relation_schema` (`row_schema.rs:58-85`) joins
`pg_catalog.pg_type t ON t.oid = a.atttypid` with no base-type recursion, so:

- a domain records the *domain's* `typname` and its own (user) namespace, so
  the `type_namespace != "pg_catalog"` gate rejects it;
- an array records `pg_catalog` + `_int8`-style `typname`, which no match arm
  covers;
- a user composite/enum records its own namespace.

The one hole a namespace check could leave — a user type planted in
`pg_catalog` — is closed by the `typname` match arms, since those eleven names
are already taken in `pg_catalog` and cannot be redefined there.

Allowlist unit coverage `payload_sidecar.rs:161-183` walks all eleven
(type, width) pairs, asserts `text` is `None`, and asserts an `int8`-named
attribute in namespace `public` is `None`.


## X5 — schema resolution fail-closed arms

`payload_sidecar.rs:41-136`. Order of gates per requested attnum:

| gate | site | rejects |
|---|---|---|
| no reloption | `:46-48` | early `Ok(None)` — no cover |
| descriptor validity | `:49` | delegates to `DistannRowSchemaDescriptor::validate` |
| vector attnum sanity | `:50-55` | `indexed_vector_attnum == 0` |
| count 1..=16 | `:56-61` | empty or oversize request |
| positive / strictly increasing | `:65-73` | duplicate, descending, zero |
| indexed vector attribute | `:74-78` | `attnum == indexed_vector_attnum` |
| absent from frozen schema | `:79-87` | attnum not present |
| dropped | `:88-92` | `attribute.dropped` |
| generated | `:93-97` | `generated_kind != 0` (covers `s` and `v`) |
| binary I/O identity | `:98-102` | empty send *or* receive function |
| type allowlist | `:103-108` | everything in X4's `None` arm |
| width overflow | `:109-111` | `checked_add` on value bytes |
| 258-byte bound | `:119-130` | value/null/total over cap |

Cross-check against packet 001 `request.md:31-38`'s rejection list (absent,
dropped, generated, indexed vector, missing binary send/receive, any type
outside the closed set): all six present, none missing, and the resolver adds
count/order/overflow gates on top. Every arm returns
`Err(String)` with an `EC_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED:` prefix — there is no silent
skip and no `unwrap`/`expect`/panic in the resolver.

`validate()` at `:49` is a second call — `resolve_relation_schema` already
calls it at `row_schema.rs:166`. Redundant, and correct: the resolver takes a
descriptor by reference and must not trust its provenance.

Attnum contiguity: `DistannRowSchemaDescriptor::validate`
(`row_schema.rs:181-189`) requires attnum == index+1 across the whole
attribute vector, so "absent from the frozen row schema" is reachable only for
`attnum > len`, and dropped attributes are still present as canonical-empty
entries. The dropped gate at `:93` therefore fires before the allowlist gate
would (a dropped attribute has empty `type_namespace`), giving the specific
error rather than the generic one. Same for the binary-I/O gate at `:98`,
which precedes the allowlist gate at `:103`.


## X6 — the 258-byte bound

`payload_sidecar.rs:5-10`:

    MAX_ATTRIBUTES        = 16
    MAX_VALUE_BYTES       = 256
    MAX_NULL_BYTES        = 2
    MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES     = 256 + 2 = 258

`:119` `null_bytes = requested_attnums.len().div_ceil(8)` → 1 for 1..=8
attributes, 2 for 9..=16. Matches the two-byte null bitmap in packet 001
`request.md:41-43`.

Arithmetic check of the stated worst case: 16 attributes x the widest allowed
scalar (`uuid`, 16 bytes) = 256 value bytes; plus `div_ceil(16, 8)` = 2 null
bytes = 258 total. That is the number packet 001 accepted, and it is below
PostgreSQL's ~2000-byte TOAST threshold as `request.md:43` claims.

Unit coverage `payload_sidecar.rs:185-199` builds a sixteen-`uuid` schema and
asserts `maximum_payload_bytes == 258`, `maximum_attribute_count == 16`,
`entry_format_version == 1`, `attributes.len() == 16`, and that
`row_schema_fingerprint` equals `schema.fingerprint().unwrap()`.

Reachability note: because the parse gate and the resolver both cap the request
at 16 attributes and the widest allowlist entry is 16 bytes, `value_bytes`
cannot exceed 256 and `maximum_payload_bytes` cannot exceed 258. The
`> MAX_*` comparisons at `:123-130` are therefore defensive and cannot be
tripped by any input reachable today. The test named
`resolution_binds_schema_and_enforces_the_258_byte_bound` proves the bound is
*attained exactly*, not that overflow errors — no negative case exists to
write. Recorded so checkpoint 2 does not treat that guard as exercised.

`maximum_attribute_count` is the format cap (16), not the resolved count.
Cross-checked against packet 001 `request.md:48`: "entry format version 1 and
the maximum attribute count" — the constant is what the accepted descriptor
contract asks for, and the per-cover attnums live in `attributes` per
`request.md:49`. Implementation matches the plan.


## X7 — T1/T2 preflight placement

`control` in both coordinators is the ec_distann index relation itself, not a
separate relation:

    src/am/ec_distann/generation_store.rs:84-114
      pub(crate) fn open_control_index(index_oid, lockmode, caller)
          -> (IndexRelationGuard, RelationHandle, DistannMetadataPage, Uuid)
        let guard = IndexRelationGuard::try_open(index_oid, lockmode)...

So t2's refactor — deleting the old
`let options = relation_options(index_relation.as_ptr());` that sat directly
above `options.validate_head_sizing_inputs()`, and hoisting
`relation_options(control.as_ptr())` to `t2.rs:223` — reads reloptions off the
same relation OID. The later
`options.validate_head_sizing_inputs()` (`t2.rs:331`) therefore observes the
same values it observed before this commit. Lock modes differ
(`ShareRowExclusiveLock` on `control` vs `AccessShareLock` on
`index_relation`), which is strictly more conservative for the reloption read,
and both are held concurrently in the same transaction.

T1 placement, `t1.rs:317-327`: after `open_control_index` +
`retain_lock_until_transaction_end` + `lock_registry_revision`, and before
`replay_registration` (`:331`), `ensure_build_slot_available` (`:348`),
`desired_participants` (`:350`), and every roster/digest/insert step.

T2 placement, `t2.rs:223-232`: after `open_control_index` +
`lock_registry_revision`, and before `replay_registration` (`:238`),
`capture_source_snapshot` (`:308`), `capture_physical_source_rows` (`:337`),
and `build_physical_graph_workspace` (`:344`).

Both sites are under the retained control lock and the registry revision row
lock, so the reloption read cannot race a concurrent registry mutation. Both
are before any source capture or physical construction, which is what
`request.md:38-40` claims.

T1/T2 binding gap (see feedback §3): nothing ties the cover T1 resolved to the
cover T2 resolves. `replay_registration` (`t1.rs:331-339`, `t2.rs:237-246`)
compares `row_schema_fingerprint` and `compatibility_digest`;
`control_compatibility_digest` (`generation_store.rs:117-141`) reads
`relation_options` but folds only `distributed_control` and `source_identity`
into its checks, not `covering_payload_attnums`. An
`ALTER INDEX ... SET (covering_payload_attnums=...)` between T1 and T2, or
before a T1 replay, changes the resolved cover with no digest movement.
Unobservable in checkpoint 1 because nothing is persisted or consumed
(`let _resolved_payload_cover` at `t1.rs:323` and `t2.rs:228`).


## X8 — no-cover behavior

`resolve_payload_cover` returns `Ok(None)` at `payload_sidecar.rs:46-48`
before touching the schema, so with no reloption the resolver itself is inert.

Two computations in the new blocks are unconditional — they run even with no
cover requested:

1. `relation_options(control.as_ptr())`. New as a direct call in t1, but
   `control_compatibility_digest` at `t1.rs:329` already called
   `relation_options(index_handle.as_ptr())` on the same relation in the same
   transaction (`generation_store.rs:132`). No new failure class.
2. `routine::indexed_ecvector_attnum(control.as_ptr())`
   (`t1.rs:319-322`, `t2.rs:225-227`). Genuinely new on the no-cover path.

Failure surface of (2), `routine.rs:729-753`:

    if info.ii_NumIndexKeyAttrs != 1 || info.ii_NumIndexAttrs > 2   -> Err
    if attnum <= 0                                                  -> Err

Each arm checked for reachability on an index that builds successfully today:

- `ii_NumIndexKeyAttrs != 1` — `amroutine.amcanmulticolumn` is not set
  (default false), and `ambuild.rs:1370-1372` errors on it at CREATE INDEX.
  Unreachable.
- `ii_NumIndexAttrs > 2` — `ambuild.rs:1373-1387` requires exactly 1 attr for
  `source_identity=none` and exactly 2 for `source_identity=include`, and
  `control_compatibility_digest` (`generation_store.rs:133-140`) requires
  `source_identity='include'` for every distributed-control index.
  Unreachable.
- `attnum <= 0` (expression key) — `resolve_identity_attribute` checks the
  INCLUDE attnum (`ambuild.rs:1389-1393`) but not the key attnum, so an
  expression-key ec_distann index is creatable. Such an index already fails
  at `indexed_ecvector_attnum` on the insert path
  (`physical_dml.rs:148`), the local read path
  (`generation_read.rs:1243`), and the scan path (`routine.rs:621`,
  `custom_scan.rs:1561`), so it was never usable. The new call moves that
  failure from first read/DML to `begin_epoch_build`, i.e. earlier and with
  the same message.

Conclusion: no index that previously produced a usable no-cover generation now
fails, and no no-cover byte, descriptor, relation, or code path changes.
`EcDistannOptions::DEFAULT` gains `covering_payload_attnums: None`
(`options.rs:363`), asserted by `options.rs:1497`
(`assert!(defaults.covering_payload_attnums.is_none())`).


